WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:11.786
In August 1942, Nazi tanks rumbled through Stavropol Territory in southwestern Russia: Germany had invaded the Soviet Union.

2
00:00:11.787 --> 00:00:22.541
Frightened peasants from one small village fled to trenches they had dug in the forest, and among them was an 11-year-old boy who would one day change the world.

3
00:00:22.542 --> 00:00:31.050
"We saw prisoners of war being executed in the street," said Mikhail Gorbachev in an October 2000 interview.

4
00:00:31.051 --> 00:00:36.056
"The war left a heavy mark on us, a painful mark."

5
00:00:36.057 --> 00:00:48.498
Hiding in the forest, Gorbachev could not have dreamed that, one day, he would become the leader of the Soviet Union and would bring democracy to a people who had never known it.

6
00:00:48.499 --> 00:01:01.837
When Gorbachev was a boy, Joseph Stalin, a dictator and the leader of the Soviet Union, strictly enforced state control over the private and public lives of Soviet citizens.

7
00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:18.227
The government of the Soviet Union was based on communism, an ideology asserting that all property should be owned by the state&#8212;as opposed to capitalism, an ideology that professed that property should be owned by individuals.

8
00:01:18.228 --> 00:01:25.876
Under Stalin, peasants were forced to give up their family farms and work on huge state farms.

9
00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:36.389
Seeking to control where and how crops were grown, Stalin disrupted the traditional farm life and many peasants starved to death.

10
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:46.645
Gorbachev was born in the village of Privolnoye on March 2, 1931, into a family who worked on one of these state farms.

11
00:01:46.646 --> 00:01:52.296
"The housing was very poor, and so were our clothes," he recalled.

12
00:01:52.297 --> 00:02:06.620
When he was 13, Gorbachev started working alongside his parents on the state farm, and at 15, he began driving a combine back and forth across the wheat fields, harvesting grain.

13
00:02:06.621 --> 00:02:19.936
He was a model student and joined the Young Communist League, an organization that taught communist values and was an important stepping-stone for any young man wishing to be a leader in the Communist Party. When

14
00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:32.418
he was 17, Gorbachev’s energy and talent earned him an Order of the Red Banner of Labor for harvesting a record crop, a rare honor for someone so young. But

15
00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:50.912
later, he would become the chief architect in the dismantling of the state farm system, and would say in a famous speech that when a person "becomes the true master of the land," he or she has a personal stake in it and uses the land more effectively. In

16
00:02:50.913 --> 00:03:00.407
1952, Gorbachev entered law school at Moscow State University and became a member of the Communist Party. "I

17
00:03:00.408 --> 00:03:08.805
remember that at the time the party’s slogans appealed to me," Gorbachev said, ". . . and I took it all on faith." His

18
00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:20.626
grandfather was a big supporter of communism and had influenced Gorbachev greatly, even though later, his grandfather was tortured, denounced as a traitor, and imprisoned. Despite

19
00:03:20.627 --> 00:03:30.463
his grandfather’s harsh treatment under Stalin, Gorbachev was inspired by communism in many ways and wanted to rise politically. By

20
00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:44.350
the time Stalin died in March 1953, he had expanded Soviet influence and communism across Eastern Europe, but at the cost of famine, war, and brutal repression. Countries

21
00:03:44.351 --> 00:03:58.261
that believed in capitalism, especially the United States, sought to prevent the spread of communism, and the struggle between these two ideologies came to be known as the Cold War. The

22
00:03:58.262 --> 00:04:17.352
next Soviet leader, Nikita Khrushchev, was critical of Stalin’s brutality and repression, and inspired thousands of young people, including Gorbachev and his wife, Raisa, to clamor for more personal freedom during the 1950s and early 1960s. Although

23
00:04:17.353 --> 00:04:33.251
this period, called the "Khrushchev thaw," only partially reversed the harsh suppression that Stalin had started, Khrushchev’s son would later write that Gorbachev’s promises of change reminded him of his father. After

24
00:04:33.252 --> 00:04:45.668
graduating with a law degree in 1955, Gorbachev went back home and held a number of leadership positions with the Young Communist League and the local Communist Party. By

25
00:04:45.669 --> 00:04:54.823
1970, he had risen to provincial party chief of Stavropol Territory, a position similar to governor. Gorbachev

26
00:04:54.824 --> 00:05:03.991
reorganized the state farms to allow peasants to have a voice in decisions and to own larger private plots of land. Soon,

27
00:05:03.992 --> 00:05:11.704
he was elected to be a member of the highest political body of the Communist Party&#8212;the Central Committee. In

28
00:05:11.705 --> 00:05:19.793
1978, Gorbachev moved his family to Moscow when he was appointed secretary of agriculture. "I

29
00:05:19.794 --> 00:05:29.533
found myself at that place at the time when society was growing ripe for . . . the desire and the expectation for change," he recalled. As

30
00:05:29.534 --> 00:05:42.117
secretary of agriculture, Gorbachev often traveled outside the Soviet Union, and he was profoundly affected by the openness and freedom that he saw abroad but not at home. Gorbachev’s

31
00:05:42.118 --> 00:05:57.920
rapid rise to the upper levels of government, coupled with his youth, made him a popular public figure, and by 1980, he was a member of the Politburo, the chief policy-making committee of the Communist Party. Then,

32
00:05:57.921 --> 00:06:06.866
in March 1985, the 54-year-old Gorbachev was elected general secretary of the Communist Party. He

33
00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:11.338
was now the leader of the Soviet Union. Gorbachev

34
00:06:11.339 --> 00:06:25.568
faced a country burdened with a weak economy, an oppressive and corrupt governmental bureaucracy, and a demoralized population, so he decided to start much-needed reforms. In

35
00:06:25.569 --> 00:06:33.389
1987, he initiated a policy he called glasnost , the Russian word for "openness."

36
00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:39.734
Long-denied freedoms, such as the right to criticize the government, were finally allowed.

37
00:06:39.735 --> 00:06:47.536
Newspapers could report the facts as they saw them, and artists and writers could enjoy the right of self-expression.

38
00:06:47.537 --> 00:07:00.374
Gorbachev reasoned that something had to be done to change the status quo: "If you simply bend in the wind and cave in under the pressure of circumstance, you will accept things as they are." Glasnost

39
00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:09.939
signaled the coming of bold change in the Soviet Union, but there was resistance from many powerful people who felt threatened. Gorbachev

40
00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:19.323
soon realized that reform could happen only from above, so he began to introduce elements of democracy into the government. He

41
00:07:19.324 --> 00:07:25.496
called this policy perestroika , which means "restructuring."

42
00:07:25.497 --> 00:07:35.443
For the first time in Soviet history, elections were held, and candidates did not have to be members of the Communist Party to run for office.

43
00:07:35.444 --> 00:07:48.411
Under perestroika, Gorbachev also introduced free-market ideas into the Soviet economy, enabling citizens to open their own businesses and keep the money they made.

44
00:07:48.412 --> 00:07:56.824
Before long, Gorbachev’s policies of perestroika and glasnost created ripple effects across Eastern Europe.

45
00:07:56.825 --> 00:08:06.493
Communist governments that had been under the thumb of the Soviet Union began to collapse, and Gorbachev made no effort to stop their fall.

46
00:08:06.494 --> 00:08:12.484
Party members, angered by such sweeping change, began to work against him.

47
00:08:12.485 --> 00:08:23.717
By 1991, Gorbachev was facing open hostility, and during an attempted coup, was even placed briefly under house arrest.

48
00:08:23.718 --> 00:08:30.226
Although the coup failed, Gorbachev’s position as the Soviet leader was severely weakened.

49
00:08:30.227 --> 00:08:38.289
On December 25, 1991, Gorbachev resigned, and the Soviet Union ceased to exist.

50
00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:52.113
Gorbachev was awarded the Nobel Prize for Peace in 1990, the year after the Berlin Wall had joyfully been torn down, reuniting democratic West Germany with communist East Germany.

51
00:08:52.114 --> 00:08:59.945
The world watched in awe as the Cold War came to an end and Russia extended a hand to the West.

52
00:08:59.946 --> 00:09:13.016
Gorbachev did not achieve all that he set out to do, but perestroika and glasnost had a lasting effect on the countries of the former Soviet Union, Eastern Europe, and the entire world.

53
00:09:13.018 --> 00:09:22.846
Mikhail Gorbachev, the son of simple peasants, had, at long last, put Russia and its people on the road to democracy.

